\tag{fdip}
\title{Realization and the information retained by observables}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{Which information survives when a finite background is replaced
by a geometric space? Assign an interval to each edge of the
[incidence presentation](fdip-0002). A triangle then distinguishes
keeping those edges from filling a face. For probability, the
[retained Gaussian law](fdip-000E) gives a sharper test: two weights
can produce the same interval but different variances at its fixed
endpoint. This is a small teaching variant of the
[weighted-cycle comparison](fdip-000I), using the existing
[fiber-constancy criterion](fdip-0042).}

\p{The [enriched background comparison](fdip-000Y) preserves
observables only when the required data and the observables are
transported together. The constructions below identify exactly what
is forgotten and separate scalar factorization from categorical
localization and gluing over covers.}

